<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuul River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tuul Gol</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuul is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> that rises in the Khentii Mountains and flows west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> to join the Orkhon. Its valley was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> in the 12th century, and later of the Tüsheet Khans. It is sacred in Mongolian tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuul-river.jpg" alt="Tuul River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidor at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuul_River_Mongolia.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
